  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 314 AM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nCool Canadian high pressure will nose into the Dakotas and \nnortheastern portions of Nebraska overnight Saturday night. \nLooking at the wind fields with this feature, northerly winds \nlook to not totally decouple across the eastern half of the \nforecast area by Sunday morning. Further west however, winds \nappear to become light and variable over the western third of \nthe forecast area late Saturday night. The latest MET and MAV \nguidance for Sunday morning are indicating upper 30s over \nnorthwestern portions of the forecast area. These numbers are \nbelow the 10% ile with the latest NBM fcst tonight. With this in\nmind, I did trend down lows in the west and northwest slightly \nSunday morning, but not as far as the MET and MAV guidance would\nsuggest. With the high pressure building into eastern Nebraska \nSunday, highs will be the coolest of the extended period with \nreadings only reaching the middle 60s in the east and around 70 \nin the west. On Monday, high pressure will build east of the \narea into the upper Mississippi valley. At the same time, a \ntrough of low pressure will deepen from eastern Wyoming into \neastern Colorado. This will place the most of the forecast area \nin a tight surface pressure gradient Monday afternoon. Based on \nthe deterministic GFS soln tonight and NBM ensembles, wind gusts\nmay approach 40 MPH in the Sandhills Monday afternoon. Gusty \nsoutherly winds will increase low level warm air advection \nacross the area beginning Monday. Highs will reach well into the\n70s to around 80 Monday afternoon. This will be followed by \neven warmer temperatures for Tuesday and Wednesday with highs \nTuesday in the upper 70s to middle 80s and mid 70s to lower 80s \nfor Wednesday and Thursday. The latest MEX guidance for North \nPlatte has a high of 93 for Tuesday, which would break the \nrecord of 91. Currently the NBM has a high of 82 which is just \nunder the 50th percentile of the NBM ensemble spread. Will need \nto monitor forecast trends especially as the GFS tonight has a \nminor ridge breakdown Tuesday which would mean the current NBM \nforecast of 82 would more than likely need to be adjusted \nhigher. As mentioned earlier, after Tuesday, temperatures will \nbe slightly cooler for Wednesday and Thursday, albeit still \naround 10 degrees above climatology for the first week of \nOctober.",
